<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel von Brukenthal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1721">1721</a>–<a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel von Brukenthal was a Transylvanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobleman and statesman who governed the Grand Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> and Joseph II from the 1770s to 1787. A collector of paintings, books and minerals, he built a baroque palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibiu">Hermannstadt</a> (Sibiu) whose collections opened to the public in 1817 as the Brukenthal Museum, one of the oldest museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-von-brukenthal.jpg" alt="Samuel von Brukenthal" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_von_Brukenthal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Samuel von Brukenthal was born on 26 July 1721 in Leschkirch (Nocrich), between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibiu">Hermannstadt</a> (Sibiu) and Agnetheln (Agnita). His grandfather and father had been royal judges. The family's original name was Brekner: his father, Michael Brekner, was ennobled in 1724 and received the noble name von Brukenthal. His mother, Susanna, belonged to the aristocratic family of Conrad von Heydendorff.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Brukenthal served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> governor of the Grand Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> from 6 July 1774 to 9 January 1787 and was a personal adviser to Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>. As governor he began building a town palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibiu">Hermannstadt</a>. Inside the medieval town the building had to keep to two demolished plots beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> church and seminary, so, unlike palaces set in parks, it was bounded by the lots it stood on.</p><p>The palace has a rectangular plan enclosing an inner courtyard, entered through a portal on the axis of its narrow side facing the street. It is counted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> buildings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, and from the start it was meant as a setting for the conservation of his art collection and antiquities. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibiu">Sibiu</a> on 9 April 1803.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The collections he gathered formed the core of the Brukenthal National Museum, which grew from a public exhibition first opened in his palace in 1817. A summer palace and park at Freck (Avrig) bear his name. At Sâmbăta de Jos he built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> residence in 1750–1760, where Count Johannes von Brukenthal later founded a Lipizzaner stud farm in 1874.</p><p>The Samuel von Brukenthal Gymnasium, across from the Protestant Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibiu">Sibiu</a>, is also named after him. First mentioned in 1380, it is the oldest German school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>; its present building dates from the 18th century, and it still teaches in German, the language of the German minority, with Romanian taught as the official language. Since the exodus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a> in the twentieth century most of its pupils have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel von Brukenthal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A gubernium answering to Vienna ran the land, sitting at Hermannstadt for much of the 18th century, with governors such as the Saxon <b>Samuel von Brukenthal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-transylvania">Grand Principality of Transylvania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nocrich, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibiu">Sibiu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.9,24.46&amp;year=1774" aria-label="Nocrich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5128 547 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Samuel von Brukenthal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5578 817h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5578 817h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.9,24.46&amp;year=1774">What was here in 1774? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
